<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	SOPS-46 / SOPS-49 / ADR 0006. Two entitlements, both required, neither
	optional:

	`com.apple.application-identifier` is what ties this binary to the App ID
	the provisioning profile was issued for. `keychain-access-groups` is what
	lets it address a keychain item at all — without it `SecItemAdd` returns
	errSecMissingEntitlement (-34018), measured on this machine.

	⚠️ NEITHER WORKS WITHOUT THE EMBEDDED PROFILE. `keychain-access-groups` is
	a restricted entitlement: a Developer ID-signed, hardened-runtime binary
	carrying it without `Contents/embedded.provisionprofile` is killed by AMFI
	before `main` runs — SIGKILL, no message, exit 137. Measured, not assumed.
	`Scripts/embed-provisioning-profile.sh` puts the profile there and fails
	the build if it cannot; `project.yml` wires the two together and says why.

	`$(AppIdentifierPrefix)` is expanded by Xcode from the profile at build
	time, so no team identifier is written down here. `codesign` on its own
	does NOT expand it — a signature made outside Xcode with this file
	verbatim carries the literal string and the binary dies exactly as if the
	entitlement were unauthorised.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.application-identifier</key>
	<string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)cz.mihalic.SopsGUI</string>
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)cz.mihalic.SopsGUI</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
